I now have a thorough picture of all 5 areas. Here is the full report.

---

# Findings: learn-p5 Codebase Exploration

## 1. Fullscreen / Maximize Preview Feature

There is **no React Native "fullscreen"** implementation. The "fullscreen" feature lives entirely inside the in-WebView exercise HTML as a `maximize-btn` that simply toggles a CSS class. The word "fullscreen" appears only in (a) p5.js reference data and (b) the button's `title` attribute — not in the actual JS behavior.

**`src/utils/editor/exerciseHtml.ts`** — the button and CSS:

- `:397-399` — the button markup, with misleading title:
```html
<button class="maximize-btn" id="maximize-btn" title="Toggle fullscreen preview">
  <span id="maximize-icon" style="font-size:16px; ...">&#x26F6;</span>
</button>
```
- `:401` — the target sketch box: `<div id="user-sketch" class="sketch-box"></div>`
- `:172-186` — the CSS that defines the "expanded" state. Note the heights are **fixed pixel values**, not viewport-relative:
```css
.sketch-box {
  height: 180px;
  background: #000000;
  ...
  transition: height 0.3s ease;
}
.sketch-box.expanded {
  height: 400px;   /* <-- hard-coded, not 100vh / 100vw */
  border-radius: 0;
}
```
- `:160-171` — `.maximize-btn` styling (just a small icon chip in the preview header, `z-index: 10`).

**`src/utils/editor/exerciseHtml.ts:1349-1363`** — the click handler (the entire "fullscreen" logic):
```js
var maximizeBtn = document.getElementById('maximize-btn');
if (maximizeBtn) {
  maximizeBtn.addEventListener('click', function() {
    var sketch = document.getElementById('user-sketch');
    var icon = document.getElementById('maximize-icon');
    if (sketch) {
      var isExpanded = sketch.classList.contains('expanded');
      sketch.classList.toggle('expanded');
      if (icon) icon.innerHTML = isExpanded ? '&#x26F6;' : '&#x2715;';
      if (!isExpanded) {
        setTimeout(function() { smoothScrollTo(sketch, 300); }, 350);
      }
    }
  });
}
```

**Why it does not cover the full viewport:** The handler only toggles the `.expanded` CSS class, which resizes the `#user-sketch` box from `180px` → `400px` tall (and never modifies width). It does **not** use the HTML Fullscreen API (`requestFullscreen`/`webkitRequestFullscreen`), nor does it set `position: fixed; inset: 0` / `100vh` / `100vw`. There is no `requestFullscreen` anywhere in `exerciseHtml.ts` (confirmed by grep — only `smoothScrollTo` matches). The sketch remains inline within the scrollable WebView document, constrained to a `400px`-tall box and the WebView's own width. To make it cover the full viewport, the handler would need either `sketch.requestFullscreen()` or a fixed-position overlay style (`position:fixed; top:0; left:0; width:100vw; height:100vh; z-index:9999`).

`smoothScrollTo` helper is defined at `:802`.

---

## 2. Run-Button Long-Press → Reference Search Popup

The "reference popup" is the **`SearchOverlay`** component (a Fuse.js-powered symbol search modal), triggered by a 600 ms long-press on the Run FAB.

### Trigger logic in the exercise view

**`src/app/learn/[course]/[id].tsx:558-590`** — Run handler + long-press timer:
```ts
const runStartTimeRef = useRef<number>(0);

const handleRun = useCallback(() => {
  if (!state.exercise) return;
  dispatch({ type: "RUN_START" });
  runStartTimeRef.current = Date.now();
  if (webViewRef.current && editorViewReady) {
    webViewRef.current.postMessage(JSON.stringify({ type: "runSketch" }));
  }
  setTimeout(() => dispatch({ type: "RUN_DONE" }), 3000);
}, [state.exercise, editorViewReady]);

const longPressTimerRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);

// Per user choice (v0.6.114): the Run-button long-press → reference search
// ...Lowered to 600ms so holding the Run button reveals the SearchOverlay...
const handleRunPressIn = useCallback(() => {
  longPressTimerRef.current = setTimeout(() => {
    longPressTimerRef.current = null;
    setSearchVisible(true);
    Haptics.impactAsync(Haptics.ImpactFeedbackStyle.Light).catch(() => {});
  }, 600);
}, []);

const handleRunPressOut = useCallback(() => {
  if (longPressTimerRef.current) {
    clearTimeout(longPressTimerRef.current);
    longPressTimerRef.current = null;
    handleRun();          // quick tap → run sketch
  }
}, [handleRun]);
```

**`src/app/learn/[course]/[id].tsx:904-930`** — the Run button JSX wiring the handlers:
```tsx
<View style={[styles.fabWrapper, { bottom: runButtonBottom }]} pointerEvents="box-none">
  <Pressable
    onPressIn={handleRunPressIn}
    onPressOut={handleRunPressOut}
    disabled={state.isRunning}
    style={({ pressed }) => [styles.runButton, { backgroundColor: ctaColor }, pressed && styles.runButtonPressed]}
    accessibilityRole="button"
    accessibilityLabel="Run sketch"
    ...
  >
    <MaterialCommunityIcons name={state.isRunning ? "reload" : "play"} size={28} color="#FFFFFF" />
  </Pressable>
</View>
```

**`src/app/learn/[course]/[id].tsx:990-996`** — the `SearchOverlay` instance rendered at the exercise-screen level (this is the "reference popup"), routing the selected symbol to `/ref?symbol=…`:
```tsx
<SearchOverlay
  visible={searchVisible}
  onClose={() => setSearchVisible(false)}
  onSelectSymbol={(name) => { router.push(`/ref?symbol=${name}`); }}
/>
```

### The popup component

**`src/components/SearchOverlay.tsx`** (190 lines) — a `Modal` with a Fuse.js fuzzy search over `P5_SYMBOLS`. Key behavior:
- `:21-33` — Fuse index built once over `P5_SYMBOLS` (`keys: name/description/module`, `threshold: 0.4`).
- `:35-38` — results: empty query shows first 20 symbols; otherwise `fuse.search`.
- `:46-58` — comment explains the imperative focus is specifically needed for "the Run-button long-press path from [id].tsx", because the Modal is always mounted and `autoFocus` only fires once; it focuses the input after a 60 ms delay so the keyboard reliably opens.
- `:53-58`:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (!visible) return;
  setQuery("");
  const t = setTimeout(() => inputRef.current?.focus(), 60);
  return () => clearTimeout(t);
}, [visible]);
```
- `:61-119` — the `Modal` UI (magnify search bar, FlatList of `name()` + module rows; selecting a row calls `onSelectSymbol(sym.name)`).

The same `SearchOverlay` is also reused in the Reference route (`src/app/ref/index.tsx:376-380` and `:462-466`) via a FAB.

---

## 3. Reference Route & Example-Code Rendering

### Route

**`src/app/ref/index.tsx`** (681 lines) — Expo Router route `/ref`. It has two modes driven by the `symbol` query param:
- **List mode** (`symbol` absent, `:355-501`): `FlatList` of `MODULE_GROUPS` (symbols grouped by module, built at `:17-25`), each row navigates to `/ref?symbol=${sym.name}` (`:434-449`).
- **Detail mode** (`SymbolDetail`, `:132-353`): shows the symbol's name, module badge, description, syntax, examples, parameters, prev/next nav, and a "View on p5js.org" link (`refUrl` at `:186`).

### Where example code is rendered (the key part)

**`src/app/ref/index.tsx:226-258`** — the examples block:
```tsx
{sym.examples && sym.examples.length > 0 && (
  <>
    <Text style={...}>Examples</Text>
    {sym.examples.map((ex, i) => (
      <View key={i} style={{ marginBottom: 16 }}>
        {sym.norender ? (
          // --- PLAIN-TEXT path: no WebView, just a static code block ---
          <View style={[styles.codeBlock, { backgroundColor: colors.surfaceDim }]}>
            <Text style={{ fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono", fontSize: 13, lineHeight: 20, color: colors.onSurface }}>
              {ex}
            </Text>
          </View>
        ) : (
          <>
            {/* Live-rendered p5 sketch in a WebView */}
            <WebView
              source={{ html: getExampleHtml(ex) }}
              style={[styles.exampleWebView, { backgroundColor: colors.surface }]}
              scrollEnabled={false}
              pointerEvents="none"
              javaScriptEnabled
              domStorageEnabled
              bounces={false}
            />
            {/* AND the source code shown as plain text below it */}
            <View style={[styles.codeBlock, { backgroundColor: colors.surfaceDim, marginTop: 8 }]}>
              <Text style={{ fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono", fontSize: 13, lineHeight: 20, color: colors.onSurface }}>
                {ex}
              </Text>
            </View>
          </>
        )}
      </View>
    ))}
  </>
)}
```

So example code is rendered **two ways** depending on the `sym.norender` flag:
- If `norender === true`: **plain text only**, in a `styles.codeBlock` (JetBrainsMono, 13px, surface-dim background). No live preview.
- Otherwise: a **live WebView** running the sketch via `getExampleHtml(ex)` (260px-tall preview) **plus** the source code as plain text in a code block beneath it.

The syntax for the "Usage" box (`:185`) is also rendered token-colored via the local `highlightSyntax` helper (`:32-73`), but the example source itself (`ex`) is rendered as a plain `<Text>` with no syntax highlighting.

Other related helpers:
- **`src/utils/editor/exampleHtml.ts`** (50 lines) — `getExampleHtml(code, colorScheme)` builds a tiny standalone HTML doc embedding `p5Source`, auto-wrapping snippet code in `setup()` if `function setup` is missing, and forcing `createCanvas(400, 400)`. The reference data comes from `src/data/reference.ts` (consuming `src/data/reference.generated.json`), which is produced by `scripts/generate-reference.mjs`.

---

## 4. How p5.js Is Bundled During Build

p5.js is **not installed from npm and is not bundled by esbuild**. It is **vendored as a minified string constant** and inlined directly into the WebView HTML at runtime.

### The vendored p5 source

**`src/utils/p5Source.ts`** (17 lines) — the entire p5.js library is stored as an array of string literals joined into `p5Source`:
```ts
// Vendored p5.js v2.3.0 - do not edit directly
// Source: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5@2.3.0/lib/p5.min.js

const _p = [
  "var p5=function(){\"use strict\";const t=Math.PI,r=\"2.3.0\",...",
  ...
  ...
];
export const p5Source: string = _p.join("");
```
So the **p5 version is v2.3.0**, hardcoded by the maintainer from the jsDelivr CDN URL `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5@2.3.0/lib/p5.min.js`. The version string also appears inside the minified source (`r="2.3.0"`).

### How it gets into a running sketch

- **`src/utils/editor/exerciseHtml.ts:2`** — imports `p5Source`.
- The exercise HTML template injects it via an inline `<script>${p5Source}</script>` tag inside the generated HTML string (the same pattern as `exampleHtml.ts:46`).
- **`src/utils/editor/exampleHtml.ts:1,46`** — reference example previews do the same:
```ts
import { p5Source } from "../p5Source";
...
<script>${p5Source}<\/script>
<script>${sketch}<\/script>
```

### Other build scripts (for context; these bundle *other* things, not p5)

- **`scripts/bundle-editor.mjs`** (41 lines) — uses **esbuild** to bundle **CodeMirror** (the code editor, NOT p5) from `src/utils/editor/bundleEntry.ts` into `src/utils/editor/codemirror-bundle.generated.ts` as `CODEMIRROR_BUNDLE`. Min size guard: 100 KB.
- **`scripts/generate-reference.mjs`** (526 lines) — clones `github.com/processing/p5.js-website` (shallow), parses the MDX reference frontmatter, and emits `src/data/reference.generated.json`. It hardcodes **`p5Version: "2.3.0"`** at `:92` (in the `metadata` block of `buildOutput`), used as the reference-data version stamp. The `WEBSITE_REPO`/`REF_DIR`/`OUT_FILE` constants are at `:7-9`.
- **`scripts/build-courses.mjs`** — compiles `src/data/courses/*.yaml` → `*.ts` (per AGENTS.md).
- Per `package.json`: `postinstall` runs `gen-reference` then `build-courses`; `start` runs `gen-reference`, `build-courses`, `bundle-editor`, then `expo start`. There is **no p5-bundling script** because p5 is vendored, not built.

### A stale/duplicate asset

**`assets/p5/p5.min.js`** exists but is **p5.js v1.11.3** (header: `/*! p5.js v1.11.3 January 22, 2025 */`), whereas the active `p5Source.ts` is **v2.3.0**. Grepping `src/` and `scripts/` for `assets/p5` or `p5.min` found **no imports/usages** — this asset appears to be unused/legacy and is not wired into any build step. (The `expo.icon`/`p5.icon` files in `assets/` are also present but unrelated to bundling the runtime.)

---

## 5. "Debugging" Settings Option & Associated Code

### The Dev Mode toggle (gate)

**`src/app/settings/index.tsx`** — the settings screen (~804 lines):
- `:37` — settings key: `devMode: "setting_devMode"`.
- `:107` — `const [devMode, setDevMode] = useState(false);`
- `:141` — loaded from AsyncStorage: `setDevMode(dev[1] === "true");`
- `:234-237` — the toggle handler:
```ts
const toggleDevMode = async (value: boolean) => {
  setDevMode(value);
  await AsyncStorage.setItem(SETTINGS_KEYS.devMode, value.toString());
};
```

### The "Debugging" UI section

**`src/app/settings/index.tsx:622-744`** — section header at `:622-623`:
```tsx
{/* Debugging */}
<SectionHeader icon="bug-outline" label="Debugging" color={derivedColors.primary} />
```
- `:624-637` — **"Dev Mode"** switch (`toggleDevMode`); description "Enable debug controls for testing".
- `:639` — everything below is wrapped in `{devMode && (<>...</>)}` so the whole Debugging toolset is conditionally revealed.
- `:641-661` — **"View Error Logs"** button → `router.push("/settings/error-logs")`.
- `:663-697` — **"Show Toast"** (`setDebugToastVisible(true)`) and **"Show Streak Toast"** (`setDebugStreakToastVisible(true)`) debug triggers.
- `:699-718` — **"Complete All Exercises"** → `handleCompleteAllExercises` (`:254-265`): writes every lesson key to `completedLessons` in AsyncStorage.
- `:720-738` — **"Reset All Progress"** → `handleResetAllProgress` (`:267-272`): removes `completedLessons` and `completedCourses`.
- `:740-748` — **"Set Streak Count"** TextInput bound to `debugStreakCount` (`:113`, default `"7"`) → `handleSetStreak` (`:274-282`): writes `streak_count` + `streak_last_visit`.
- `:111-113` — debug state vars: `debugToastVisible`, `debugStreakToastVisible`, `debugStreakCount`.
- `:791-800` — the actual debug `Toast`/`StreakToast` modals rendered from these flags:
```tsx
<Toast visible={debugToastVisible} message="Debug toast — component rendering works!" ... />
<Toast visible={debugStreakToastVisible} ... />  // via StreakToast
```

### The Error Logs route (opened from Debugging)

**`src/app/settings/error-logs.tsx`** (276 lines) — `expo-router` route `/settings/error-logs`:
- `:9-14` — `ErrorLogEntry` shape: `{ timestamp, route, error, stack? }`.
- `:23-32` — on mount, reads `AsyncStorage.getItem("error_log")` (reversed so newest first).
- `:34-37` — `handleClearLogs` removes the `"error_log"` key.
- `:45-91` — detail view (when a log row is tapped): error/route/timestamp, a "Show/Hide Stack" toggle, and a "Copy Stack Trace" button that writes the stack to AsyncStorage key `"clipboard"`.
- `:94-148` — list view: FlatList of log entries with a "Clear All Logs" footer and an empty-state ("No errors logged").

### Where errors are actually written (the producers of the `"error_log"` key)

Errors are appended to the `"error_log"` AsyncStorage key in **two places** in the exercise view, `src/app/learn/[course]/[id].tsx`:
- `:378-388` — exercise **load failure** path:
```ts
const logEntry = {
  timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
  route: `/learn/${course}/${id}`,
  error: errMsg,
  stack: errStack,
};
const existing = await AsyncStorage.getItem("error_log");
const logs = existing ? JSON.parse(existing) : [];
logs.push(logEntry);
await AsyncStorage.setItem("error_log", JSON.stringify(logs.slice(-20)));
dispatch({ type: "LOAD_ERROR", error: errMsg });
```
- `:466-479` — **runtime sketch error** path, from a WebView `sketchError` message:
```ts
case "sketchError":
  if (msg.error) {
    (async () => {
      const logEntry = {
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
        route: `/learn/${course}/${id}`,
        error: `[Sketch] ${msg.error}`,
        context: msg.container || "unknown",
      };
      const existing = await AsyncStorage.getItem("error_log");
      const logs = existing ? JSON.parse(existing) : [];
      logs.push(logEntry);
      await AsyncStorage.setItem("error_log", JSON.stringify(logs.slice(-20)));
    })();
  }
```
Both cap the log buffer to the last 20 entries (`logs.slice(-20)`). Note: there is **no global error boundary** writing to `"error_log"` — `src/app/_layout.tsx` has no `ErrorBoundary`/`error_log` references (grep confirmed); only the exercise screen captures load and sketch-runtime errors.

---

## Summary Table

